var tafsirData_59 = {
  "1": "\"Whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth glorifies Allah. For He is the Almighty, All-Wise.\" opens Surah al-Hashr exactly as Surah al-Hadid began, establishing continuity. The words repeat that whatever is in heavens and earth already declares Allah free from imperfection, reminding the listener that revelation and law that follow are from the same Glorifier who is Almighty and All-Wise, the manager of heavens, earth, drop, seed, water, and fire.\n\nRepeating \"Whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth\" after a chapter that detailed private whispers, alliances, and the wall between light and darkness shows that even conspiratorial hearts operate inside a creation that praises its Lord. Secret talks do not exit His kingdom, just as strongholds do not put anyone out of His reach.\n\n\"For He is the Almighty, All-Wise\" again pairs irresistible might with perfect wisdom. That pairing prepares for the narrative of Banu an-Nadir, whose strongholds seemed mighty yet were overcome by wise decree. Almighty explains that expulsion could occur despite their defenses; All-Wise explains that it occurred with lessons, with mercy for believers, and with distribution of gains that prevents wealth merely circulating among the rich.\n\nThe opening is not decorative. It frames the historical lesson that follows as an act of the Praised who deserves glorification, not a tribal anecdote. Taking a lesson, as the coming verse commands, means seeing beyond politics to the Almighty who humbles defiance and the All-Wise who legislates after it.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to hear \"glorifies\" as merely poetic. The Quran treats it as real worship by creation in ways we do not fully comprehend, inviting humans not to be the only silent party. Remembering universal glorification before recounting expulsion encourages humility when recounting history. It is Allah who removes, who casts horror, and who judges, while human actors are witnesses and agents within His allowing. That awareness keeps the coming lessons from hardening into triumphalism and keeps them as glorification that softens hearts after long time and revives them like earth after rain.",
  "2": "“He is the One Who expelled the disbelievers of the People of the Book from their homes for their first banishment ever. You never thought they would go. And they thought their strongholds would put them out of Allah's reach. But the decree of Allah came upon them from where they never expected. And He cast horror into their hearts so they destroyed their houses with their own hands and the hands of the believers. So take a lesson from this, O people of insight!” opens Surah al-Hashr with the account of the expulsion of the Banu Nadir, and the surah takes its name from the “hashr” — the gathering, the banishment — that the verse describes. “He is the One Who expelled the disbelievers of the People of the Book from their homes for their first banishment ever,” or “huwa alladhi akhraja allatheena kafaroo min ahli al-kitabi min diyarihim li-awwali al-hashr.” Ibn Kathir relates the background with the detail the surah assumes: the Banu Nadir were one of the Jewish tribes of Medina, bound to the Prophet by a pact of peace and mutual support, and they broke the pact and plotted against him, and when their treachery was exposed, the Prophet ordered their expulsion, and they left their homes and their strongholds and went into exile, the first banishment of its kind in that land. Al-Tabari notes the word “li-awwali al-hashr” — “for the first gathering” — and explains that this was the first time they were gathered and driven out, to be followed later by a second expulsion under the caliph Umar, so that the verse records the beginning of a judgment that would complete itself.\n\nThe verse then states what the Banu Nadir themselves had thought and what actually happened: “You never thought they would go. And they thought their strongholds would put them out of Allah's reach. But the decree of Allah came upon them from where they never expected,” or “ma thanantum an yakhrujoo wa thannoo annahum mani'atuhum husoonuhum min Allahi fa-atahumu Allahu min haythu lam yahtasiboo.” Ibn Kathir explains that neither the believers nor the Banu Nadir themselves expected the expulsion, and the Banu Nadir trusted in their fortresses, their walls, and their strength, and the decree of God came to them from the direction they had not calculated — from within their own hearts — for the next words say, “And He cast horror into their hearts so they destroyed their houses with their own hands and the hands of the believers,” or “wa qathafa fee quloobihimu ar-ru'ba yukhriboona buyutahum bi-aydeehim wa aydi al-mu'mineen.” Al-Razi observes the depth of the lesson, for the strongest fortress is no defense when the heart within it is afraid, and the Banu Nadir, who trusted in their walls, were undone by the fear that God cast into their hearts, so that they tore down their own homes with their own hands, and the believer is taught that the real fortress is the heart, and that the heart which God protects is safer than every wall.\n\nThe verse closes with the summons that the surah intends: “So take a lesson from this, O people of insight!” or “fa'tabiroo ya uli al-absar.” Al-Qurtubi explains that the lesson is addressed to those with insight — “uli al-absar” — the ones who see with the eye of the heart, for the outward event of the expulsion is a sign whose meaning is read only by those who look beneath the surface. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to read the fall of the strong as a lesson in the limits of strength, and to know that the decree of God reaches the one who trusts in walls from the direction he never expected, and the believer is taught to build his security in the heart with God, not in the wall without.",
  "3": "“Had Allah not decreed exile for them, He would have certainly punished them in this world. And in the Hereafter they will suffer the punishment of the Fire.” states what would have befallen the Banu Nadir had the exile not been decreed, and the verse places the mercy of the exile beside the severity of the alternative. “Had Allah not decreed exile for them, He would have certainly punished them in this world. And in the Hereafter they will suffer the punishment of the Fire,” or “wa law la an kataba Allahu 'alayhimu al-jala'a la-'adhdhabahum fi ad-dunya wa lahum fi al-akhirati 'adhabu an-nar.” Ibn Kathir explains that the exile — “al-jala'” — was itself the lighter sentence, for had God not written the expulsion for them, He would have punished them in this world with slaughter and captivity, and the verse teaches that the exile, bitter as it was to the Banu Nadir, was a mercy in comparison with what might have been, and the believer is taught that the decrees of God are weighed in a scale that the sufferer does not see. Al-Tabari notes that the word “kataba” — “He decreed,” “He wrote” — declares that the exile was a matter written by God, and the believer is taught that the events of the world are written before they happen, and that the writing is the wisdom.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the two punishments, for the worldly punishment is limited and the Hereafter's punishment is lasting, and the verse places them side by side so that the believer may measure the one against the other, and the believer is taught that the worldly punishment, severe as it may be, is the lesser, and that the punishment of the Fire is the greater, and that the fear of the greater should move the believer to avoid the cause of both. The verse also teaches the believer that the mercy of God is hidden in His decrees, for the exile which the Banu Nadir lamented was the decree that spared them a worse fate in this world, and the believer is taught to receive the decrees of God with trust, knowing that the Lord who decreed the lighter may have spared the heavier. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to measure the trials of this world against the punishments of the next, and to find the trials light by the comparison, and to thank God for the trials that are lighter than the sins deserve.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to see the mercy in the severity, for the exile of the Banu Nadir was the mercy that withheld the sword, and the believer is taught to trust the wisdom of God in the shape of His decrees, and to know that the Hereafter is the true scale, and that the one who is spared in this world but not in the next has been spared nothing.",
  "4": "“This is because they defied Allah and His Messenger. And whoever defies Allah, then Allah is truly severe in punishment.” states the cause of the punishment in a single sentence, and the verse grounds the whole account in the principle it illustrates. “This is because they defied Allah and His Messenger. And whoever defies Allah, then Allah is truly severe in punishment,” or “thalika bi-annahum shaqqoo Allaha wa rasoolahu wa man yushaqqi Allaha fa-inna Allaha shadeedu al-'iqab.” Ibn Kathir explains that the word “shaqqoo” — “they defied,” “they opposed,” “they split away” — describes the Banu Nadir's breaking of their pact and their opposition to God and His messenger, and the verse declares that the exile was the consequence of the defiance, not an act of aggression against them, and the believer is taught that the punishments of God are not arbitrary but the answers to the deeds that preceded them. Al-Tabari notes the word “shaqaqa” — which carries the sense of splitting and opposing — for the Banu Nadir had split away from the pact and opposed the messenger, and the believer is taught that the defiance of God is the splitting of the bond between the servant and his Lord, and that the punishment is the consequence of the split.\n\nAl-Razi observes the principle that the verse states in its second half, for the punishment of the Banu Nadir is made an instance of the general law — “whoever defies Allah, then Allah is truly severe in punishment” — and the believer is taught to read the particular event as the illustration of the universal truth, and to know that the severity of God in punishment is the other side of His mercy in warning, for the severity is not the beginning but the answer to the defiance that refused the warning. The verse also teaches the believer the seriousness of opposing the messenger, for the defiance of the messenger is the defiance of the One who sent him, and the believer is taught to honor the messenger as the bond that ties him to God, and to know that the cutting of the bond is the cutting of the safety. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to examine his own relation to God and His messenger, and to guard against the defiance that splits the bond, for the severity of God in punishment is as sure as His mercy in guidance.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to fear the severity of God through the severity of His warning, and to know that the one who defies God and His messenger has defied the only refuge, and that the severity which follows is the severity of the one who was defied, and the believer is taught to keep the bond with God unbroken.",
  "5": "“Whatever palm trees you believers cut down or left standing intact, it was all by Allah's Will, so that He might disgrace the rebellious.” addresses an incident that arose during the siege of the Banu Nadir, and the verse settles the question that the cutting of the palm trees had raised. “Whatever palm trees you believers cut down or left standing intact, it was all by Allah's Will, so that He might disgrace the rebellious,” or “ma qata'tum min leenatin aw taraktumooha qa'imatan 'ala usooliha fa-bi-idhni Allahi wa li-yukhziya al-fasiqeen.” Ibn Kathir explains the occasion: during the siege of the Banu Nadir, the Muslims cut down some of the palm trees of the enemy and left others standing, and the Banu Nadir reproached the Muslims, saying that the Prophet, who forbade corruption, was now allowing the cutting of trees, and the verse was revealed to declare that the cutting and the leaving were both by the permission of God, and that the purpose of both was the disgrace of the rebellious. Al-Tabari notes that the verse answered the reproach of the enemy, for the cutting of the trees in war was not corruption but a lawful means of pressing the siege, and the believer is taught that the rules of war are set by God, not by the enemy's complaints.\n\nAl-Razi observes the wisdom of the phrase “by Allah's Will,” for the verse traces both the cutting and the leaving to the permission of God, and the believer is taught that the events of the struggle are in the hand of God, and that the believer acts within the permission and leaves the outcome to the One who permitted. The verse also states the purpose — “so that He might disgrace the rebellious,” or “li-yukhziya al-fasiqeen” — for the cutting of the trees was one of the means by which the enemy was humbled, and the believer is taught that the disgrace of the rebellious is the disgrace of their defiance, and that the humiliation of the enemy in this world is a foretaste of their humiliation in the next. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to obey the commands of God in the midst of struggle, to cut and to leave as God wills, and to know that the struggle is governed by the permission of God and aimed at the disgrace of the rebellious, and the believer is taught to keep his acts within the permission.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to see the hand of God in the details of the struggle, for even the cutting of a palm tree and the leaving of it are by the will of God, and the believer is taught to act with the confidence of one who is within the permission, and to know that the purpose of the struggle is the disgrace of the rebellious and the honoring of the truth.",
  "6": "“As for the gains Allah has turned over to His Messenger from them—you did not even spur on any horse or camel for such gains. But Allah gives authority to His messengers over whoever He wills. For Allah is Most Capable of everything.” introduces the ruling on the gains that came to the Muslims from the Banu Nadir, and the verse teaches a principle of the law of war and booty. “As for the gains Allah has turned over to His Messenger from them — you did not even spur on any horse or camel for such gains. But Allah gives authority to His messengers over whoever He wills. For Allah is Most Capable of everything,” or “wa ma afa'a Allahu 'ala rasoolihi minhum fa-ma awjaftum 'alayhi min khaylin wa la rikabin wa lakinna Allaha yusallitu rusulahu 'ala man yasha'u wallahu 'ala kulli shay'in qadeer.” Ibn Kathir explains the occasion and the ruling: the property of the Banu Nadir was not won by battle or by the charging of horses, for the Muslims had not fought for it, and it was therefore “fay'” — property that God turned over to His messenger without fighting — and its distribution was the Prophet's to command, and the believer is taught the distinction between the booty won by fighting and the “fay'” granted without it. Al-Tabari notes the word “afa'a” — “He turned over,” “He restored” — for the property of the enemy, when granted without battle, is the grant of God, and the believer is taught that the wealth of the world is distributed by the hand of God, and that the distribution follows His command.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the word “awjaftum” — “you spurred” — for the verse reminds the believers that they did not hasten horses or camels for this gain, and the believer is taught that the gains which come without effort are the purest test of gratitude, for the gain that costs nothing is the gain that the heart may most easily take for granted, and the believer is taught to receive the grants of God with the gratitude that the effortlessness should deepen rather than dull. The verse also teaches the believer the power of God over the distribution of the world, for God gives His messengers authority over whom He wills, and the believer is taught that the authority of the messenger is the authority of the Sender, and that the wealth which the messenger distributes is distributed by the command of God. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to obey the distribution of the messenger, for the messenger distributes by the command of God, and the believer is taught to receive his portion with contentment and to give of it as he is commanded.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to see the property of the enemy as the property of God, granted and withdrawn by His hand, and the believer is taught to hold the wealth of the world as a trust whose distribution is the Lord's, and to know that the One who grants without effort is the One who will ask about the granting.",
  "7": "“As for gains granted by Allah to His Messenger from the people of other lands, they are for Allah and the Messenger, his close relatives, orphans, the poor, and needy travellers so that wealth may not merely circulate among your rich. Whatever the Messenger gives you, take it. And whatever he forbids you from, leave it. And fear Allah. Surely Allah is severe in punishment.” states the distribution of the gains and the principle that governs it, and the verse is one of the foundations of the Islamic law of public wealth. “As for gains granted by Allah to His Messenger from the people of other lands, they are for Allah and the Messenger, his close relatives, orphans, the poor, and needy travellers so that wealth may not merely circulate among your rich,” or “ma afa'a Allahu 'ala rasoolihi min ahli al-qura fa-lillahi wa lir-rasooli wa li-dhi al-qurba wal-yatama wal-masakeeni wabni as-sabeeli kay la yakoona doolatan bayna al-aghniya'i minkum.” Ibn Kathir explains that the verse fixes the categories of those entitled to the “fay'” — the gains granted without battle — and the believer is taught that the wealth of the community is not the property of the rich alone, and that the categories named — the relatives, the orphans, the poor, the wayfarer — are the categories of those whom the wealth is meant to reach, so that the wealth circulates among all and not only among the rich. Al-Tabari notes the phrase “kay la yakoona doolatan bayna al-aghniya'” — “so that it may not become a thing circulating among the rich” — and the believer is taught that the principle of the distribution is the prevention of the concentration of wealth, and that the law of God is a law of balance.\n\nAl-Razi observes the depth of the principle, for the verse is not merely a ruling about one battle but a statement of the philosophy of wealth in the community of faith, and the believer is taught that wealth is a trust to be distributed with justice, and that the community in which wealth circulates only among the rich is the community that has departed from the law of God. The verse then closes with the command that reaches every sphere of life: “Whatever the Messenger gives you, take it. And whatever he forbids you from, leave it. And fear Allah. Surely Allah is severe in punishment,” or “wa ma atakumu ar-rasoolu fa-khuthoohu wa ma nahakum 'anhu fantahoo wattaqoo Allaha inna Allaha shadeedu al-'iqab.” Al-Qurtubi explains that this closing command makes the messenger the authority in what is given and what is forbidden, and the believer is taught that the obedience to the messenger is the obedience to God, and that the fear of God is the root of both the taking and the leaving.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to receive the commands of the messenger as the commands of God, to take what the messenger gives and to leave what he forbids, and to know that the distribution of wealth and the commands of the law are all of a piece, the one obedience to the one Lord, and the believer is taught to fear God in the taking and the leaving, for the severity of God is the other side of the mercy of His law.",
  "8": "“Some of the gains will be for poor emigrants who were driven out of their homes and wealth, seeking Allah's bounty and pleasure, and standing up for Allah and His Messenger. They are the ones true in faith.” names the first of the categories entitled to the gains, and the verse honors the emigrants with a description that is their title in the Quran. “Some of the gains will be for poor emigrants who were driven out of their homes and wealth, seeking Allah's bounty and pleasure, and standing up for Allah and His Messenger. They are the ones true in faith,” or “lil-fuqara'i al-muhajireena allatheena ukhrijoo min diyarihim wa amwalihim yabtaghoona fadlan min Allahi wa ridwanan wa yansuroona Allaha wa rasoolahu ula'ika humu as-sadiqoon.” Ibn Kathir explains that the verse is about the Muhajirun, the emigrants who left Mecca and their homes and their wealth for the sake of God, and the believer is taught that the emigrants are the poor whose poverty is their honor, for they gave up the world and were given the promise of God. Al-Tabari notes the three descriptions — they were driven out of their homes and wealth, they sought the bounty and pleasure of God, and they stood up for God and His messenger — and the believer is taught that these three are the marks of the true believer, the one who loses for God, seeks from God, and strives for God.\n\nAl-Razi observes the word “as-sadiqoon” — “the true in faith” — for the verse seals the description of the emigrants with the title of truthfulness, and the believer is taught that the truthfulness of faith is not the truthfulness of the tongue but the truthfulness of the life, the truthfulness that is shown in the leaving of the home and the seeking of the bounty and the standing for the messenger, and the believer is taught to measure his own faith by the measure of the emigrants. The verse also teaches the believer the honor of the poor for God's sake, for the emigrants were poor and were yet the first of the entitled, and the believer is taught that poverty in the path of God is a title, not a shame, and that the poor who gave up the world for God are the rich in the sight of God. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to honor the emigrants and to learn from them the meaning of sacrifice, for the emigrants are the models of the faith that leaves the world and seeks the Lord.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to see in the emigrants the pattern of the true believer, and to ask whether his own faith has the marks that the verse names — the leaving for God, the seeking of God, and the standing for God — and the believer is taught to make his life an emigration from the world to the Lord.",
  "9": "“As for those who had settled in the city and embraced the faith before the arrival of the emigrants, they love whoever immigrates to them, never having a desire in their hearts for whatever of the gains is given to the emigrants. They give the emigrants preference over themselves even though they may be in need. And whoever is saved from the selfishness of their own souls, it is they who are truly successful.” turns to the second of the categories, and the verse honors the Ansar — the helpers of Medina — with the description of their selflessness that became a proverb in the faith. “As for those who had settled in the city and embraced the faith before the arrival of the emigrants, they love whoever immigrates to them, never having a desire in their hearts for whatever of the gains is given to the emigrants. They give the emigrants preference over themselves even though they may be in need,” or “wallatheena tabawwa'oo ad-dara wal-eemana min qablihim yuhibboona man hajara ilayhim wa la yajidoona fee sudoorihim hajatan mimma ootoo wa yu'thiroona 'ala anfusihim wa law kana bihim khasasah.” Ibn Kathir explains that the verse is about the Ansar, who had established the home of faith and settled in the faith before the emigrants came, and the believer is taught that the Ansar loved the emigrants and gave them preference over themselves even in their own need, and the word “ithar” — the giving of preference to another over oneself — is the virtue that the verse immortalizes. Al-Tabari notes the phrase “wa law kana bihim khasasah” — “even though they may be in need” — for the preference of the Ansar was not the preference of the rich who give from surplus but the preference of the needy who give from need, and the believer is taught that this is the highest degree of generosity.\n\nAl-Razi observes the depth of the description, for the verse names three things — the settling in the city, the embracing of the faith before the emigrants, and the love of the emigrants — and the believer is taught that these three are the marks of the true helper, the one whose home is the home of faith and whose heart is without envy and whose hand gives preference to the other. The phrase “never having a desire in their hearts” teaches the believer that the test of generosity is in the heart, for the one who gives without envy is the one who has purified his heart, and the believer is taught to purify his heart of the desire for what others have been given. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to practice “ithar,” to give the other preference over himself, and to know that the preference of the other over the self is the summit of the faith, and the believer is taught to be of the Ansar in his own community, the one who helps and prefers.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to learn the virtue of “ithar” from the Ansar, and to know that the community of faith is built upon the selflessness that the verse describes, and the believer is taught to give preference to his brother over himself, and to find in the giving the joy that the verse immortalized.",
  "10": "“And those who come after them will pray, ’Our Lord! Forgive us and our fellow believers who preceded us in faith, and do not allow bitterness into our hearts towards those who believe. Our Lord! Indeed, You are Ever Gracious, Most Merciful.’” records the prayer of the third generation of the community, and the verse teaches the believer the bond that ties the generations of faith together. “And those who come after them will pray, 'Our Lord! Forgive us and our fellow believers who preceded us in faith, and do not allow bitterness into our hearts towards those who believe. Our Lord! Indeed, You are Ever Gracious, Most Merciful,'” or “wallatheena ja'oo min ba'dihim yaqooloona rabbana ighfir lana wa li-ikhwanina allatheena sabaqoona bil-eemani wa la taj'al fee quloobina ghillan lillatheena amanoo rabbana innaka ra'oofun raheem.” Ibn Kathir explains that the verse is about those who came after the emigrants and the Ansar — the later generations of the believers — and the believer is taught that the later generations are joined to the earlier ones by the prayer for forgiveness, and that the community of faith is one community across the centuries, each generation praying for the ones before and leaving the door open for the ones after. Al-Tabari notes the prayer against “ghill” — the bitterness, the rancor, the hidden malice — and the believer is taught that the purity of the heart toward the believers is the condition of the community's unity, and that the prayer for the removal of bitterness is the prayer that preserves the bond.\n\nAl-Razi observes the structure of the three verses, for the surah has named the emigrants, the Ansar, and now those who came after, and the believer is taught that the three are the three generations of the faith, and that the third generation is defined not by its place but by its prayer — the prayer for the forgiveness of the predecessors and the purity of the heart toward them — and the believer is taught to make that prayer his own, and to know that the community which prays for its past and guards its heart against bitterness is the community that remains one. The prayer also teaches the believer the manner of the relationship with those who preceded, for the later generation does not criticize the earlier but prays for its forgiveness, and the believer is taught to honor the predecessors and to ask forgiveness for them and for himself. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to purify his heart of rancor toward the believers, for the rancor is the disease that breaks the community, and the believer is taught to pray the prayer of the verse, and to know that the One addressed — the Ever Gracious, the Most Merciful — is the One who answers.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to live the prayer of the verse, to forgive and to seek forgiveness, to guard his heart against bitterness, and to know that the community of faith is the community of the pure-hearted, and the believer is taught to be of those who come after and pray, and to leave the same prayer for those who come after him.",
  "11": "“Have you O Prophet not seen the hypocrites who say to their fellow disbelievers from the People of the Book, ’If you are expelled, we will certainly leave with you, and We will never obey anyone against you. And if you are fought against, we will surely help you.’? But Allah bears witness that they are truly liars.” turns from the believers to the hypocrites, and the verse exposes the promises that the hypocrites made to the Banu Nadir. “Have you O Prophet not seen the hypocrites who say to their fellow disbelievers from the People of the Book, 'If you are expelled, we will certainly leave with you, and We will never obey anyone against you. And if you are fought against, we will surely help you'? But Allah bears witness that they are truly liars,” or “a-lam tara ila allatheena nafaqoo yaqooloona li-ikhwanihimu allatheena kafaroo min ahli al-kitabi la-in ukhrijtum la-nakhrujanna ma'akum wa la nutee'u feekum ahadan abadan wa in qootiltum la-nansurannakum wallahu yashhadu innahum la-kathiboon.” Ibn Kathir explains that the verse names the leader of the hypocrites, Abdullah ibn Ubayy and his company, who sent word to the Banu Nadir promising to stand with them and to leave with them if they were expelled, and the believer is taught that the hypocrite is the one who promises the disbelievers and the believers both, and who stands with neither when the hour comes. Al-Tabari notes the emphatic oath of their promises — “we will certainly leave,” “we will never obey,” “we will surely help” — and the emphatic witness of God that they are liars, and the believer is taught that the witness of God is the final judgment on the promises of the hypocrite.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the verse for the believer's understanding of the hypocrite, for the hypocrite is the one whose tongue speaks promises and whose heart is elsewhere, and the believer is taught that the promises of the hypocrite are the promises of the fearful, made to both sides and kept to neither, and the believer is taught to judge by the witness of God and not by the words of men, and to know that the one whom God witnesses to be a liar is a liar whatever his oaths. The verse also teaches the believer the danger of relying on the promises of the hypocrite, for the Banu Nadir relied on the promises of the hypocrites and were abandoned, and the believer is taught to rely on God and not on the promises of the wavering. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to keep his own promises, for the promise is a trust, and the believer is taught to be the opposite of the hypocrite, whose promises are lies and whose help is nothing.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to recognize the hypocrite by his promises, for the promise that is made to everyone is the promise that is kept to no one, and the believer is taught to keep his tongue in agreement with his heart, and to know that the witness of God sees both the tongue and the heart.",
  "12": "“Indeed, if they are expelled, the hypocrites will never leave with them. And if they are fought against, the hypocrites will never help them. And even if the hypocrites did so, they would certainly flee, then the disbelievers would be left with no help.” states what the promises of the hypocrites were worth, and the verse answers their oaths with the reality that God knew. “Indeed, if they are expelled, the hypocrites will never leave with them. And if they are fought against, the hypocrites will never help them. And even if the hypocrites did so, they would certainly flee, then the disbelievers would be left with no help,” or “la-in ukhrijoo la yakhrujoona ma'ahum wa la-in qootiloo la yansuroonahum wa la-in nasaroohum la-yuwallunna al-adbara thumma la yunsaroon.” Ibn Kathir explains that the verse declares the hollowness of the hypocrites' promises, for if the Banu Nadir were expelled the hypocrites would not leave with them, and if they were fought the hypocrites would not help them, and even if the hypocrites did come to their help, they would turn their backs and flee, and the believer is taught that the promise of the hypocrite is a promise without substance, and that the one who relies on it relies on the wind. Al-Tabari notes the three steps of the verse — they will not leave, they will not help, and if they help they will flee — and the believer is taught that the verse leaves the promise of the hypocrite no corner in which to hide.\n\nAl-Razi observes the precision of the verse in exposing the cowardice of the hypocrite, for the verse says that even the help of the hypocrite, if it were given, would be the help of the fleeing, and the believer is taught that the helper who flees is no helper, and that the coward's help is a burden, and the believer is taught to seek the help that stands and to be of the helpers who do not flee. The verse also teaches the believer the fate of those who rely on the wavering, for the Banu Nadir relied on the promises of the hypocrites and were left with no help, and the believer is taught to place his reliance on God, whose help does not flee and whose promise does not fail. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to be a helper whose help is real, for the community of faith is built upon the help that stands, and the believer is taught to fear the state of the one whose help is only a promise, and to make his own help a fact.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to know the difference between the promise of the sincere and the promise of the hypocrite, for the sincere promises what he does and the hypocrite promises what he does not, and the believer is taught to be of the sincere, and to trust that the help of God is the help that does not flee.",
  "13": "“Indeed, there is more fear in their hearts for you believers than for Allah. That is because they are a people who do not comprehend.” diagnoses the fear of the hypocrites, and the verse exposes the misplaced dread that governed their hearts. “Indeed, there is more fear in their hearts for you believers than for Allah. That is because they are a people who do not comprehend,” or “la-antum ashaddu rahbatan fee sudoorihim min Allahi thalika bi-annahum qawmun la yafqahoon.” Ibn Kathir explains that the verse addresses the believers, telling them that the hypocrites and the disbelievers fear the believers more than they fear God, and the believer is taught that this is the measure of the heedless heart, which fears the visible power of men and does not fear the invisible power of God, and that the fear which should be God's is given to His creatures. Al-Tabari notes the word “rahbah” — “fear,” “dread” — and the believer is taught that the heart is made to fear God, and that when the fear is misplaced and given to men, the heart has lost its compass and its Lord.\n\nAl-Razi observes the cause that the verse names — “they are a people who do not comprehend” — for the fear of men over the fear of God is the fruit of the failure to comprehend, and the believer is taught that the comprehension of the heart is the knowledge that the power of men is nothing before the power of God, and that the one who comprehends this fears God and is not shaken by men, while the one who does not comprehend fears men and is not shaken by God. The verse also teaches the believer the source of true courage, for the believer who fears God alone is the believer who is not afraid of men, and the believer is taught to place his fear where it belongs, and to let the fear of God cast out every lesser fear. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to examine his own fears, for the fear that is given to creatures rather than to the Creator is the sign of the heart that has not comprehended, and the believer is taught to comprehend, and to fear God alone.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to be a source of the fear that the verse describes, for the believers whom the heedless fear are the believers whose fear is given to God, and the believer is taught to live in the fear of God so that the fear of men may not touch him, and to know that the courage of the believer is the fruit of his fear of the One.",
  "14": "“Even united, they would not dare fight against you except from within fortified strongholds or from behind walls. Their malice for each other is intense: you think they are united, yet their hearts are divided. That is because they are a people with no real understanding.” describes the disunity that lay beneath the apparent unity of the enemy, and the verse teaches the believer to see through the outward show of strength. “Even united, they would not dare fight against you except from within fortified strongholds or from behind walls. Their malice for each other is intense: you think they are united, yet their hearts are divided. That is because they are a people with no real understanding,” or “la yuqatiloonakum jamee'an illa fee quran muhassanatin aw min wara'i judurin ba'suhum baynahum shadeed tahsabuhum jamee'an wa quloobuhum shatta thalika bi-annahum qawmun la ya'qiloon.” Ibn Kathir explains that the verse describes the enemy — the disbelievers and the hypocrites together — as a force that appears united but is divided within, and the believer is taught that the enemy who appears one is in fact many, and that the appearance of unity is the illusion that the verse shatters. Al-Tabari notes the two images — the fighting from within fortresses and from behind walls — and the believer is taught that the enemy who dares not meet the believers in the open is the enemy whose strength is the strength of the wall, not the strength of the heart.\n\nAl-Razi observes the contrast the verse draws between the outward and the inward, for the verse says “you think they are united, yet their hearts are divided,” and the believer is taught that the unity of the enemy is the unity of the flesh while their hearts are scattered, and that the strength of a community is the strength of its hearts, not the strength of its numbers or its walls. The verse then names the cause — “they are a people with no real understanding” — and the believer is taught that the division of the heart is the fruit of the failure to understand, for the heart that understands is the heart that unites upon the truth, and the heart that does not understand is the heart that scatters upon the falsehood. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to build the unity of the heart, for the community of faith is strong by the unity of its hearts, and the believer is taught to guard against the division that the verse describes, and to know that the enemy's division is the believer's opportunity and the believer's warning.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to see through the outward show of the enemy, for the enemy who appears united may be divided within, and the believer is taught to trust the unity of the believers, which is the unity of the heart upon the truth, and to know that the divided hearts of the enemy are the prelude to their fall.",
  "15": "“They are both just like those who recently went down before them: they tasted the evil consequences of their doings. And they will suffer a painful punishment.” compares the Banu Nadir and their allies to those who had gone before them, and the verse places the present defeat in the line of the past defeats. “They are both just like those who recently went down before them: they tasted the evil consequences of their doings. And they will suffer a painful punishment,” or “kamathali allatheena min qablihim qareeban thaqoo wabala amrihim wa lahum 'adhabun aleem.” Ibn Kathir explains that the verse likens the Banu Nadir and the hypocrites to those who had preceded them in defying the Prophet — the Quraysh at Badr, or the Banu Qaynuqa' — and the believer is taught that the defeat of the defiers is a repeated story, and that the taste of the evil consequence of one's doings is the law that governs every defier. Al-Tabari notes the word “wabal” — “evil consequence,” “burden” — for the doings of the defiers returned upon them as a burden and a punishment, and the believer is taught that the deed returns upon its doer, and that the consequence of the deed is the deed's own fruit.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the comparison, for the verse teaches the believer to read the present in the light of the past, and to know that the fate of the defiers is one fate, and that the defier of today is the defier of yesterday in a new dress, and the believer is taught to take heed from the predecessors rather than to repeat their deeds and their end. The verse then adds the punishment of the Hereafter — “and they will suffer a painful punishment” — and the believer is taught that the worldly consequence is not the whole of the account, and that the Hereafter holds the punishment that the world only began to taste, and the believer is taught to fear the full account and not only its first installment. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to measure his deeds by their consequences, for the deed carries its consequence within it, and the believer is taught to choose the deeds whose consequences are mercy.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to see the law of consequences at work in the world, for the defiers of every age have tasted the evil of their doings, and the believer is taught to place himself on the side of the deeds whose taste is good, and to know that the taste of this world is the foretaste of the next.",
  "16": "“They are like Satan when he lures someone to disbelieve. Then after they have done so, he will say on Judgment Day, ’I have absolutely nothing to do with you. I truly fear Allah—the Lord of all worlds.’” likens the hypocrites to Satan, and the verse draws the comparison that exposes the whole relation between the tempter and the tempted. “They are like Satan when he lures someone to disbelieve. Then after they have done so, he will say on Judgment Day, 'I have absolutely nothing to do with you. I truly fear Allah — the Lord of all worlds,'” or “kamathali ash-shaytani idh qala lil-insani ukfur fa-lamma kafara qala innee baree'un minka innee akhafu Allaha rabba al-'alameen.” Ibn Kathir explains that the verse compares the hypocrites, who lured the Banu Nadir to defiance and then abandoned them, to Satan, who lures a man to disbelief and then, when the man has disbelieved, disowns him, and the believer is taught that the tempter is always the first to abandon the tempted, and that the one who leads another to ruin will not share the ruin but will stand apart from it. Al-Tabari notes the irony of Satan's words — “I truly fear Allah” — for the tempter who called to disbelief now claims to fear God, and the believer is taught that the fear of Satan is a lie, and that the claim of the tempter is the last of his deceptions.\n\nAl-Razi observes the depth of the comparison, for the verse teaches the believer the nature of every temptation, and that the tempter — whether Satan or the hypocrite or the desire — promises company and delivers abandonment, and the believer is taught to see the end of the temptation at its beginning, and to know that the one who leads him to sin will not stand with him in the punishment. The verse also teaches the believer the loneliness of the wrongdoer at the end, for the wrongdoer who followed the tempter finds the tempter disowning him, and the believer is taught to choose the company that will not disown, the company of God and His messengers and the believers. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to recognize the tempter in whatever form he comes, and to know that the tempter's friendship is the friendship of the hunter with the prey, and the believer is taught to flee the temptation and to cling to the truth.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to see in the disowning of Satan the end of every false friendship, and to know that the only friendship that endures is the friendship with God, and the believer is taught to make his friendship with the One who does not disown, and to refuse the tempter who will abandon him at the end.",
  "17": "“So they will both end up in the Fire, staying there forever. That is the reward of the wrongdoers.” closes the comparison with the end of both the tempter and the tempted, and the verse seals the account with the sentence that gathers them into the Fire. “So they will both end up in the Fire, staying there forever. That is the reward of the wrongdoers,” or “fa-kana 'aqibatahuma annahuma fi an-nari khalidayni feeha wa thalika jaza'u ath-thalimeen.” Ibn Kathir explains that the end of both the tempter and the tempted is the Fire, and the believer is taught that the tempter does not escape by disowning the tempted, for the disowning of the tempter is itself a lie, and the two — the one who lured and the one who followed — share the one end. Al-Tabari notes the word “'aqibah” — “the final outcome,” “the consequence” — for the verse is stating the final outcome of the relation that the previous verse described, and the believer is taught that the outcome of the tempter's friendship and the tempted's following is the Fire, and that the outcome is the true measure of the relation.\n\nAl-Razi observes the word “khalidayni” — “abiding,” “staying forever” — for the end of the tempter and the tempted is not a passing punishment but an abiding one, and the believer is taught that the deeds whose end is the Fire are the deeds of the wrongdoers, and that the word “ath-thalimeen” — “the wrongdoers” — is the verdict that names both the tempter and the tempted, for the tempter wronged the tempted and the tempted wronged himself, and the believer is taught to flee the wrong that leads to the Fire. The verse also teaches the believer the justice of the reward, for the end is called “the reward of the wrongdoers,” and the believer is taught that the Fire is not an arbitrary punishment but the reward that the wrong has earned, and that the wrong carries its reward within it. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to see the end of sin at its beginning, for the sin whose end is the Fire is the sin that should never be begun, and the believer is taught to measure his deeds by their ends.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to read the account of the hypocrites and their allies to its end, and to know that the end of the tempter and the tempted is one, and that the believer is taught to choose the end of the believers, the Garden, by choosing the company and the deeds that lead to it.",
  "18": "\"O believers! Be mindful of Allah and let every soul look to what deeds it has sent forth for tomorrow. And fear Allah, for certainly Allah is All-Aware of what you do.\" shifts from the joint doom of the wrongdoing alliance to a direct call for individual accountability. The address \"O believers\" shows that even after praising emigrants, helpers, and later supplicants, every soul still needs self-examination.\n\n\"Be mindful of Allah\" translates \"ittaqu Allah,\" maintaining reverent awareness that Allah hears the woman's plea, knows whatever is in heavens and earth, is with you wherever you are, and keeps account while people forget. Mindfulness is the positive counterpart to Satan causing forgetting of remembrance. It restores the balance sent so people may administer justice and keeps private talk oriented to goodness and righteousness.\n\n\"Let every soul look to what deeds it has sent forth for tomorrow\" uses a striking metaphor. \"Tomorrow\" is the next life, whose Day is the appointed Day, the tomorrow to today's today. Deeds are portrayed as ahead, sent forth like advance provision or investment, like wealth that does not merely circulate among the rich but is forwarded to the eternal treasury. This echoes the good loan that is multiplied and the charity men and women that receives honourable reward. The verse asks each soul to audit that forward shipment. What has my soul already sent? Quality matters more than show. Sincere, hidden charity, truthful oaths, and making room in gatherings are quietly sent ahead where the Record preserves them.\n\n\"And fear Allah, for certainly Allah is All-Aware of what you do\" repeats fear as reverent caution and grounds it in perfect awareness. The All-Aware sees both sent deeds and omitted duties, and will inform all on the Day when all are gathered.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to read this as anxiety-provoking self-obsession. The intended fear is constructive, leading to hope, as the next verse contrasts forgetting Allah with remembering Him, and as earlier promise of double mercy, light, and forgiveness follows fear and belief. Practically, looking to what is sent for tomorrow can be done nightly, reviewing speech, charity, and covenant-keeping, and then choosing one concrete good to send forward next morning, trusting that the reviver of dead earth can revive a dull soul to look ahead wisely.",
  "19": "\"And do not be like those who forgot Allah, so He made them forget themselves. It is they who are truly rebellious.\" warns against the opposite of being mindful and looking to what is sent for tomorrow. After commanding mindfulness and self-audit, the verse describes a group whose forgetting led to self-loss.\n\n\"Those who forgot Allah\" includes neglecting remembrance, ignoring clear proofs, dismissing the balance of justice, and treating the noble Quran lightly after it was sent from the Lord of all worlds. Forgetting here is not simple memory lapse, but willful disregard, as when hypocrites chose to be tempted, awaited demise of believers, doubted, and were deluded by false hopes until death's decree came.\n\n\"So He made them forget themselves\" states the consequence. When Allah is forgotten, concern for one's own soul, benefit, and ultimate home is lost. A person forgets what deeds are being sent for tomorrow, forgets that wealth and children will not help against Allah, and forgets that ransom will not be accepted. Self-forgetting is profound. A person may meticulously manage wealth's circulation among the rich or guard strongholds, yet forget the self that will stand alone when the soul reaches the throat and is nearer to Allah than onlookers can see.\n\n\"It is they who are truly rebellious\" translates \"humu al-fasiqun,\" those who persistently deviate from the path. Rebellion here is not sporadic lapse, but settled departure from the covenant already taken. The label follows the diagnosis rather than preceding it, showing that rebellion is both cause and effect of forgetting.\n\nThis verse mirrors the earlier diagnosis that Satan has taken hold of them, causing them to forget remembrance, and that Satan's party is bound to lose. Forgetting Allah invites satanic takeover; remembrance repels it.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to think self-exaltation equals self-care. The verse shows that true self-care requires Allah-remembrance. Ignoring Him while chasing play, amusement, luxury, boasting, and competition in wealth and children appears as self-assertion, but ends as self-forgetting.\n\nFor daily renewal, cultivate deliberate remembrance. Humble the heart when Allah is remembered, revive the heart like revived earth, and check regularly what the soul has sent forward, so that forgetting does not settle and the soul remains present to itself before it is asked about what it did.",
  "20": "\"The residents of the Fire cannot be equal to the residents of Paradise. Only the residents of Paradise will be successful.\" states a foundational inequality after calling believers to be mindful and not to forget Allah. The verse makes explicit what the preceding contrasts implied between Satan's party and Allah's party, between debasement and elevation, between torment and grace, and between strongholds that fail and balance that sustains.\n\n\"The residents of the Fire cannot be equal to the residents of Paradise\" denies equivalence. \"Residents\" again suggests abiding dwelling, not passing visit. Residents of the Fire are those who remained with defiance, hypocrisy, and hindering until death without return, those for whom severe, humiliating punishment was prepared and for whom ransom will not be accepted. Residents of Paradise are the party of Allah, those in whose hearts faith was instilled and who were strengthened with spirit, who made room, gave the good loan, and asked for forgiveness without bitterness. Placing them as equals would betray justice, wisdom, and the writing in the Record where every calamity and blessing was inscribed before creation. The well-preserved Record guards distinction precisely to render it on the Day when all matters are returned.\n\n\"Only the residents of Paradise will be successful\" defines true success, or \"fawz,\" as the attainment of forgiveness and pleasure of Allah and a Garden as vast as heavens and earth, not as worldly play, luxury, or competition won in this life that withers to chaff. The earlier verse \"No calamity... without being written\" was given so people would not grieve excessively over missed worldly wins or boast over gains, because real winning is elsewhere. \"Only\" stresses exclusivity. Those who imagined they succeeded through cunning alliances and shielding oaths will find success did not accompany them beyond the wall, whose one side is grace and the other torment.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to use this verse to excuse neglecting worldly duties, as if work and family are irrelevant because only Paradise matters. The Quran praises iron with benefits, planting, and kind dealings, but subordinates them to ultimate success.\n\nPractically, this verse invites recalibration. Measure decisions by whether they send forward deeds for the tomorrow that determines lasting residence, joining those who remember rather than forget, who prefer others despite need, and who seek a success that the Almighty, All-Wise has prepared and promised to multiply.",
  "21": "\"Had We sent down this Quran upon a mountain, you would have certainly seen it humbled and torn apart in awe of Allah. We set forth such comparisons for people, so perhaps they may reflect.\" gives a powerful image of the Quran's weight immediately after defining ultimate success. The verse moves from future residences to present revelation's majesty.\n\n\"Had We sent down this Quran upon a mountain\" uses a mountain, the archetype of stability and mass, the same kind of mountain described earlier as humbled and torn apart would be at the thought of being entrusted with guidance. In Surah al-Waqi'ah, mountains were crushed to scattered dust at the Inevitable Event. Here a mountain is imagined as recipient of the Quran before that Day, showing that its words carry a gravity that would shake even the unshakeable.\n\n\"You would have certainly seen it humbled and torn apart in awe of Allah\" describes the mountain's response. Humbled, or \"khashi'an,\" is bowed, lowered, and subdued in reverence, not in despair. Torn apart, or \"mutasaddi'an,\" is split, cracked open under the impact of awe. Awe here is \"khashyat Allah,\" reverent fear that recognizes true greatness and responds with softness. The same hardness that afflicted human hearts after long time is contrasted with a mountain that would soften instantly before the same revelation that some humans take lightly or repay with denial.\n\n\"We set forth such comparisons for people, so perhaps they may reflect\" explains method. Quranic parables translate unseen weight into familiar images, like plants that delight then wither to chaff, or transactions like a good loan. Comparison invites reflection, \"yatafakkarun,\" deep thinking that leads to action, not mere appreciation of eloquence.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to read this as praise of dramatic emotional display alone. The mountain's imagined humility is instructive; human reflection should lead to concrete responses already commanded, being humbled at remembrance rather than hardened, reviving the heart like revived earth, and lending the good loan.\n\nFor practice, approaching the Quran as a mountain would, with humility and readiness to be moved, transforms recitation. Instead of rushing, pause, allow awe to soften, and let that softness shape charity, honesty in gatherings, and remembrance that protects from Satan's takeover and prepares for the day when only residents of Paradise are truly successful.",
  "22": "\"He is Allah—there is no god worthy of worship except Him: Knower of the seen and unseen. He is the Most Compassionate, Most Merciful.\" opens the sequence of magnificent divine names that close Surah al-Hashr. After the humbling comparison of the Quran and the mountain, the passage turns to the One who sent the Quran, summarizing lordship, knowledge, and mercy.\n\n\"He is Allah—there is no god worthy of worship except Him\" states pure monotheism, or \"tawhid,\" after a chapter that exposed alliances built on fear of people more than fear of Allah and loyalty to displeased peoples. No stronghold, wealth, child, oath, or party deserves the devotion owed to the Owner of the kingdom.\n\n\"Knower of the seen and unseen\" translates \"'Alim al-ghayb wa al-shahadah,\" knowledge that encompasses the absent and present, the secret talk of three where He is fourth and the open proclamation in gatherings where room is made or withheld. \"Seen\" includes the house destroyed with hands visible and the charity given openly; \"unseen\" includes what is hidden in the heart, the light prepared ahead and to the right, and the wall with a gate whose one side is grace and other torment. This knowledge underwrites the well-preserved Record where every calamity or blessing was written before creation and supports the promise that on the Day He will inform them of what they did.\n\n\"He is the Most Compassionate, Most Merciful\" closes with \"al-Rahman al-Rahim,\" the same pair that opened glorification as knower of mercy. After chapters that warned of severe, humiliating punishment, these names remind that divine knowledge is exercised with encompassing mercy. Compassion reaches all creation with provision, rain, fire, and guidance to bring from darkness to light. Mercy specifically embraces those who humble hearts, revive them, and lend good loans, granting double share, light, and forgiveness.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think mercy cancels the severe punishment warned about. The Quran holds both, showing mercy as the invitation and severe punishment as the outcome for persistent, knowing defiance that insists on denial until death.\n\nRemembering this verse in worship and decision helps balance awe and hope. The Knower sees private deeds, so sincerity matters more than show, and the Most Compassionate, Most Merciful is the One to whom every soul should look for what it has sent forth for tomorrow, trusting that humbled reflection will be met with gracious response.",
  "23": "\"He is Allah—there is no god except Him: the King, the Most Holy, the All-Perfect, the Source of Serenity, the Watcher of all, the Almighty, the Supreme in Might, the Majestic. Glorified is Allah far above what they associate with Him in worship!\" continues the litany of beautiful names, expanding from knowledge and mercy to sovereignty and perfection. The opening again stresses \"no god except Him,\" anchoring after calls not to forget Allah and not to equate residents of Fire and Paradise.\n\nEach title carries weight. \"The King\" translates \"al-Malik,\" absolute owner whose kingdom of heavens and earth is not borrowed or contested, unlike the temporary claims of Banu an-Nadir's strongholds. \"The Most Holy\" translates \"al-Quddus,\" free from any defect, deficiency, or impurity that touches creation. \"The All-Perfect\" translates \"al-Salam,\" the source of peace, wholeness, and safety, free from flaw and the giver of soundness to hearts that remember. \"The Source of Serenity\" is another facet of \"al-Mu'min,\" the granter of security and the confirmer of truth, who secures believers and confirms His messengers with clear proofs. \"The Watcher\" translates \"al-Muhaymin,\" the guardian and observer who oversees everything, knowing whatever goes into earth and ascends, the One who kept account while they forgot.\n\n\"The Almighty, the Supreme in Might, the Majestic\" translates \"al-'Aziz, al-Jabbar, al-Mutakabbir,\" a triad of irresistible power, compulsive authority that mends and compels, and greatness that is truly majestic. Al-Jabbar also carries the tender sense of mending the broken, relevant to a chapter that mended community through fair distribution and prayer without bitterness.\n\n\"Glorified is Allah far above what they associate with Him in worship\" closes with \"subhana Allah 'amma yushrikun,\" declaring flawlessness beyond partners. After exposing alliances with displeased peoples and oath-shields that associate others' favor with Allah's, this glorification cleanses monotheism.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to recite these names as mere poetry without trust. Knowing Him as King, Holy, Peace, Watcher, and Mighty should affect private talk, oaths, and room-making, as one lives under the Watcher who is also the Source of Peace, inviting reliance rather than grieving whispers.",
  "24": "\"He is Allah: the Creator, the Inventor, the Shaper. He alone has the Most Beautiful Names. Whatever is in the heavens and the earth constantly glorifies Him. And He is the Almighty, All-Wise.\" seals Surah al-Hashr by focusing on origination, beauty, and universal praise. The closing mirrors the opening \"Whatever in heavens and earth glorifies Allah, for He is the Almighty, All-Wise,\" forming a perfect frame.\n\n\"The Creator, the Inventor, the Shaper\" translates \"al-Khaliq, al-Bari', al-Musawwir,\" three stages of bringing into being. Creator is the One who measures and decrees creation. Inventor is the One who brings it into existence without prior model, free from defect, unlike human copies that need prototypes. Shaper is the One who fashions each thing with its distinct form, from the mountain that would be torn apart in awe to the seed that becomes a plant that delights then withers to chaff, to the human shaped from a drop and destined to be gathered for the appointed Day. Together they answer those who doubted resurrection after dust and bones, showing the same fashioning power that first shaped can reshape in forms unknown.\n\n\"He alone has the Most Beautiful Names\" affirms that no beautiful quality belongs to another at its source. Beautiful names include the King, the Holy, the Peace, and the earlier Most Compassionate, Most Merciful, each beautiful and perfect in meaning, unlike human titles that exaggerate or fade.\n\n\"Whatever is in the heavens and the earth constantly glorifies Him\" returns to continuous praise, now as present ongoing act. Heavens praised earlier in past tense, now glorify continuously, linking the mountain's imagined humility to the constant reality of cosmic worship that humans are invited to join.\n\n\"And He is the Almighty, All-Wise\" reaffirms the pair that makes mighty action wise. From expulsion without horses to gains distributed so wealth does not circulate only among the rich, might was guided by wisdom.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think \"Shaper\" concerns only physical form. Shaping includes provision of law, balance, and purposeful tests like iron with benefits to prove who stands for Him. Remembering these three names fosters gratitude when shaped in ease and patience when shaped in hardship, confident that the Shaper who glorifies the heavens through praise will glorify repentant hearts through revival."
};
